[2055.207, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I tested every assumption against the running page in headless Chrome. The biggest finding: **the first\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  version was a real seizure risk**. Its moving slits and per-frame flicker produced 15 flashes in one second\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  in part of the screen, against a limit of 3 under the standard web accessibility guideline (WCAG). The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  current version produces none.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | # | Assumption, riskiest first | How I tested it | Result |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 1 | Pointer guidance works, including on touch | Simulated mouse moves and presses, plus a tap on a phone-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sized screen | **Pass.** The spike cluster follows the pointer, pressing makes it bigger, and a tap changes\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  about 137k pixels. In the first version a tap changed **0** pixels: a tap without dragging did nothing. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 2 | The loop is exact again once the pointer goes idle | Interacted, waited 6 s, then compared with a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  session nobody touched | **Pass:** 0 pixels differ. The first version had 12 differing pixels. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 3 | 60 fps holds everywhere | Timed each image's full draw at four screen sizes | **Failed, now fixed.**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  At 2560×1440 on a high-density screen, a typical draw took 29 ms (worst 93 ms), too slow for a 60 fps\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  screen. Fixes are below. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 4 | Flicker is photosensitive-safe | Brightness measured in 16 screen regions, 240 samples across the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  loop, scored against WCAG's flash rule | My first test used the wrong measure and \"failed\" at 44.5%. With\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the real rule: the brightest step is 0.040 against a 0.1 threshold, with 0 flashes. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 5 | The Matisse window is actually visible | Pixel-compared against a copy with the window removed |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Pass:** it shows in 12 of 12 sampled frames, about 12k pixels each (0.3% of the image), so it stays hidden\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  but findable. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 6 | The \"reveal the field\" overlay renders | Pressed F and looked at the frame | **Pass:** contour lines\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  appear, with gold rings where spikes start to form |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 7 | Reduced motion turns off flicker and flashes | Ran 120 frames with the setting on | **Pass:** flash\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and shutter dip are both 0. The first version ignored the setting and still flashed at up to 0.368 opacity.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 8 | Changing the shader didn't change the picture | Pixel-compared against step 3, after checking the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  renderer gives identical output on repeat runs | 7,257 pixels differed. A copy with the new branch present\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  but never used gave the same 7,257, so it's compiler rounding at highlight edges, not a change in behaviour.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 9 | three.js always loads from the CDN | Pointed the page at a broken link | **Failed, now fixed.** The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  page used to go silently blank. It now says what it needs: \"three.js could not load. Check the connection\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and reload.\" The same goes for a browser with WebGL turned off. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What changed in the page**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Faster drawing:** most of the pool is below the strength where spikes form, so it now skips the spike\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  calculation entirely.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Resolution cap:** drawing is capped at about 8 Mpx, roughly 1.5× density on a 1440p screen. Under the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  drum slats the softening is barely visible.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Together these bring the typical draw at 2560×1440 down from **29 ms to 15 ms** (worst case 93 ms to 30\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ms).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Smaller fixes:** the load-failure message from row 9, and the safety comment in the code now cites the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  WCAG measure I actually ran.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What's still open:** the worst-case draw (30 ms) still sometimes exceeds one 60 fps frame. This machine's\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  graphics chip is a virtual one, so these timings only stand in for real hardware.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The test scripts are in `.ambition/tools/` (`challenge.js` runs most of the table, and `eq.js` does the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  version-to-version pixel check). A snapshot of this version is in `.ambition/steps/4/`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2055.381, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 5 of pushing the work further: Range. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 5/8 · Range`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2108.692, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 5/8 · Range\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What it's really trying to do, as verbs.** It has to:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - stop a scrolling stranger in the first second;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - make a liquid seem alive and answer the hand;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - make orange and violet *sing* as structure, not decoration;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - make stepped frames feel like a crafted optical toy rather than lag;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - loop without a seam;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - reward a second look with hidden intent;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - leave viewers sure a mind made it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The fields, each with the method I'd borrow and the direction it proposes:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Field | Method actually borrowed | Direction it proposes |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Generative / shader artist** (the genre's own best practitioner) | Every frame is a poster: choose the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  composition, don't inherit it | Make the fluid the hero of the frame, not a speck in a big dish |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Macro liquid photographer** (ferrofluid film work, high-speed liquid art) | A low, grazing camera with a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  bright backdrop, so spikes read as silhouettes against reflected light | Low angle: black spikes against an\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  orange mirror-horizon |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Character animator** (the 12 principles; animating \"on twos\") | Smear frames on the snap, plus follow-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  through | One stretched \"smear\" frame on each pull, then a follow-through frame |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Victorian optical-toy maker** (the field I wouldn't normally consider) | The *crank-up*: a zoetrope\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  starts slow and comes up to speed, and the image wakes as it does | An entrance. The pool starts as a flat\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  mirror, the frames stutter in slowly, then speed up as the spikes erupt. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Riso printmaker** (considered, not taken) | Two-ink plates misregistered each frame | Turned down: it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  would bring a third look that competes with the liquid one |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Red team:** accessibility and harm auditor | Tries to break and misuse it, and asks who it could harm |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  See below |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The edge I took:** the portfolio scroller chooses it for *first-second wow* and *it answers my hand*. So\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'm taking the photographer's framing, the toy-maker's crank-up and the animator's smear, and fixing\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  everything the red team finds.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Red team findings, from reading the code against how it will be used:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **Scroll trap (a real harm when embedded in a portfolio).** `touch-action:none` on the body means a phone\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  user who swipes over an iframe of this piece can't scroll past it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Battery and heat.** It renders constantly even when scrolled offscreen in an embed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **Screen readers.** The canvas is anonymous, with no description of what it is.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  4. **Keyboard.** The reveal button has no visible focus state.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  5. **Motion sensitivity.** Already handled by reduced motion. The new entrance must respect it too.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Applying all of it:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
