[3369.614, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I missed the stretch target, but the attempt made the page sharper and faster on high-density screens, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it still passes all 23 acceptance checks.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The target:** draw each image in ≤ 4 ms (typical) and ≤ 6 ms (slowest 10%) at 2560×1440 on a 2× display,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  at full 14.7 Mpx resolution. That's about 9× harder than the 12 ms step 6 needed for a capped 8 Mpx. The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  fallback was step 6 as it stood.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Where it broke:** this machine can't draw an empty full-resolution frame in 4 ms.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Variant at 5120×2880 | Typical | Slowest 10% |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Flat plane, trivial shaders, anti-aliasing on (the floor) | 10 ms | 14 ms |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Same, anti-aliasing off | **5 ms** | 6 ms |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Full shader, anti-aliasing off, one surface solve per pixel | 8 ms | 10 ms |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Full shader, anti-aliasing off, three solves per pixel (kept) | 10–11 ms | 12–14 ms |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The 4 ms target sits below even the 5 ms no-anti-aliasing floor. The graphics chip here is a virtual one, so\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  real hardware may do better.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I tried, in order:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **Profiling first.** The surface shader turned out not to be the main cost. The multisample anti-aliasing\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  buffer cost about 8 ms on its own at that size.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Normals from screen-space derivatives** (one solve per pixel): reached 8 ms, but a 1:1 crop showed\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  staircase shading and harsher speckle. I reverted it, because the fluid looked worse.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **Skip anti-aliasing on 2× displays, keep the smooth three-solve normals, raise the pixel budget to about\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  15 Mpx:** kept.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What changed in the deliverable:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **On 2× displays:** 2560×1440 now renders at the full 5120×2880 in about 11 ms. Step 6 needed 12–15 ms for\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  a capped 3771×2121. That's 1.8× the pixels for slightly less time.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **On 1× displays:** unchanged, at 7 ms.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Seam fix:** the stretch also turned up a defect present since step 3. Each spike's cone was wider than\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  its hexagonal cell, so the surface jumped at cell borders, making dark dashes at the spike bases. I clamped\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the cone to the cell, and the 1:1 crop shows fewer dashes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Still open:** the far clusters still glitter, because spikes smaller than a pixel catch the sharp\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  highlights. Every version so far has it, including step 6.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The profiling variants are in `.ambition/stretch/`, and the 1:1 crop comparisons are\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `.ambition/shots/s1.png` (derivative normals, rejected) and `.ambition/shots/s2.png` (kept). A snapshot of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  this version is in `.ambition/steps/7/`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
